Can you use the following models to create summaries of each in stargazer, while also presenting the coefficients on the natural scale:
model1 <- lm(data = cis_clean, formula = log(hourly_wage) ~ province + 
              pop_level + age_level + female + full_time + imm)

model2 <- lm(data = cis_clean, formula = log(hourly_wage) ~ province + 
              pop_level + age_level + female + full_time + 
               highest_ed_level + imm)

model3 <- lm(data = cis_clean, formula = log(hourly_wage) ~ province + 
              pop_level + age_level + female + full_time + 
               highest_ed_level + imm_length_level + imm)

### ChatGPT ###
Yes, you can use the stargazer package in R to present model summaries, and you can exponentiate the coefficients to present them on the natural (non-log) scale. Since your dependent variable is log(hourly_wage), exponentiating the coefficients will show multiplicative effects on hourly_wage.

However, stargazer does not directly support transforming coefficients, so we need to first manually exponentiate the coefficients and confidence intervals, and then use stargazer with these manually formatted results.

Here's a complete workflow in R:

Step-by-step: Transforming and Presenting the Models
r
Copy
Edit
library(stargazer)
library(broom)

# Fit the models
model1 <- lm(data = cis_clean, formula = log(hourly_wage) ~ province + 
              pop_level + age_level + female + full_time + imm)

model2 <- lm(data = cis_clean, formula = log(hourly_wage) ~ province + 
              pop_level + age_level + female + full_time + 
              highest_ed_level + imm)

model3 <- lm(data = cis_clean, formula = log(hourly_wage) ~ province + 
              pop_level + age_level + female + full_time + 
              highest_ed_level + imm_length_level + imm)

# Function to exponentiate coefficients and CIs
get_exp_coefs <- function(model) {
  tidy_model <- broom::tidy(model, conf.int = TRUE)
  tidy_model$estimate <- exp(tidy_model$estimate)
  tidy_model$conf.low <- exp(tidy_model$conf.low)
  tidy_model$conf.high <- exp(tidy_model$conf.high)
  return(tidy_model)
}

exp_model1 <- get_exp_coefs(model1)
exp_model2 <- get_exp_coefs(model2)
exp_model3 <- get_exp_coefs(model3)

# Display transformed models in stargazer
# We override the coefficient values with the exponentiated ones

# Create coefficient and SE vectors for each model
coefs1 <- exp_model1$estimate
se1 <- (exp_model1$conf.high - exp_model1$conf.low) / (2 * 1.96)

coefs2 <- exp_model2$estimate
se2 <- (exp_model2$conf.high - exp_model2$conf.low) / (2 * 1.96)

coefs3 <- exp_model3$estimate
se3 <- (exp_model3$conf.high - exp_model3$conf.low) / (2 * 1.96)

# Generate stargazer table with exponentiated coefficients
stargazer(model1, model2, model3,
          coef = list(coefs1, coefs2, coefs3),
          se = list(se1, se2, se3),
          type = "text", # or "latex", "html"
          title = "Regression Results (Exponentiated Coefficients)",
          dep.var.labels = "Hourly Wage (Multiplicative Effects)",
          digits = 3)
Notes:
The standard errors are approximated from the confidence intervals; this is acceptable when only the exponentiated output is needed.

exp(coef) means: a coefficient of 1.10 implies a 10% increase in hourly wage for a one-unit change in the predictor.

If you'd prefer the table in LaTeX or HTML, just change the type parameter.

