### User ###
How can I mention province controls without adding them in the list of coefs

### ChatGPT ###
Great question. In regression tables, it's common to indicate the inclusion of control variables (like fixed effects or dummies) without listing all their individual coefficients. With stargazer, you can do this by:

Suppressing specific coefficients and noting their inclusion:
Step 1: Suppress coefficients by name using omit argument
To avoid showing province coefficients, you can use a regular expression in the omit argument to hide them.

r
Copy
Edit
stargazer(model1, model2, model3,
          coef = list(coefs1, coefs2, coefs3),
          se = list(se1, se2, se3),
          omit = c("province"),  # hides any term starting with "province"
          omit.labels = "Province Controls",
          type = "text",
          title = "Regression Results (Exponentiated Coefficients)",
          dep.var.labels = "Hourly Wage (Multiplicative Effects)",
          digits = 3)
omit = "province" matches any term that starts with "province" — this works well if you're using dummy variables named like provinceON, provinceQC, etc.

Step 2: Customize omit.labels to mention "Province Controls Included"
This label will appear in the table in place of the omitted variables — letting readers know they're part of the model, just not displayed.

Optional: Add a footnote
You can also clarify further with a manual note:

r
Copy
Edit
notes = "Exponentiated coefficients. Province fixed effects included but not displayed."
Add this to your stargazer call like:

r
Copy
Edit
stargazer(..., notes = "Exponentiated coefficients. Province fixed effects included but not displayed.")
